## Summary

Allow classes that extend a generic base class to reference the generic type parameters (like `TOptions`) of the base class in their methods or properties **without needing to repeat them** or redefine them locally.

## Use Case

When building reusable data mappers, I often define classes like:

```ts
type UserAddressMapperOptions = { userId: string };

class UserAddressMapper extends Mapper<UserAddress, UserAddressFirestoreData, UserAddressMapperOptions> {
  getCollectionPath(options: UserAddressMapperOptions): string {
    return `users/${options.userId}/addresses`;
  }
}
```

The problem is that I must repeat UserAddressMapperOptions in both:

* The type argument to the base class

* The method parameter

This feels redundant and violates DRY principles, especially when I already passed the generic type to the base class.

## Desired Behavior
It would be great if TypeScript allowed accessing the generic type from the base class, something like:

```ts
class UserAddressMapper extends Mapper<UserAddress, UserAddressFirestoreData, { userId: string }> {
  getCollectionPath(options: this["TOptions"]): string {
    return `users/${options.userId}/addresses`;
  }
}
```

or perhaps:

```ts
class Mapper<TLocal, TRemote, TOptions> {
  type TOptions = TOptions; // exposed to subclasses
}

class UserAddressMapper extends Mapper<UserAddress, UserAddressFirestoreData, { userId: string }> {
  getCollectionPath(options: super.TOptions): string { ... }
}
```

## Benefits
Avoid repeating types

Cleaner subclass API

Promotes DRY and reusability

Helps advanced patterns like DSL mappers or repositories

## Notes
This is not the same as wanting to reintroduce the generic in the subclass (<TOptions>), but rather being able to reference what was already supplied to the base class.

## Related
[TypeScript#5863](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/issues/5863) — Ability to extract base class type parameter in derived class

[TypeScript#7534](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/issues/7534) — Suggestion for accessing base type generics

Thanks for considering!

### ✅ Viability Checklist

- [x] This wouldn't be a breaking change in existing TypeScript/JavaScript code
- [x] This wouldn't change the runtime behavior of existing JavaScript code
- [x] This could be implemented without emitting different JS based on the types of the expressions
- [x] This isn't a runtime feature (e.g. library functionality, non-ECMAScript syntax with JavaScript output, new syntax sugar for JS, etc.)
- [x] This isn't a request to add a new utility type: https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/wiki/No-New-Utility-Types
- [x] This feature would agree with the rest of our Design Goals: https://github.com/Microsoft/TypeScript/wiki/TypeScript-Design-Goals

### ⭐ Suggestion

## Desired Behavior
It would be great if TypeScript allowed accessing the generic type from the base class, something like:

```ts
class UserAddressMapper extends Mapper<UserAddress, UserAddressFirestoreData, { userId: string }> {
  getCollectionPath(options: this["TOptions"]): string {
    return `users/${options.userId}/addresses`;
  }
}
```

or perhaps:

```ts
class Mapper<TLocal, TRemote, TOptions> {
  type TOptions = TOptions; // exposed to subclasses
}

class UserAddressMapper extends Mapper<UserAddress, UserAddressFirestoreData, { userId: string }> {
  getCollectionPath(options: super.TOptions): string { ... }
}
```

### 📃 Motivating Example

Good news for TS writers to avoid repeating yourself — you can now reference inherited generic types like directly in subclass methods.

TypeScript now lets you reference generic type parameters from a base class directly in subclass methods — no need to repeat them! This makes it easier to write DRY, maintainable code in patterns like mappers, repositories, and generic services. You can now use the types you've already declared without duplicating them across your class.


### 💻 Use Cases

### 1. What do you want to use this for?

Avoid repeating types across subclass methods that already inherit them from the base class.  
This would allow for:

- Cleaner subclass APIs  
- Promoting DRY and type reusability  
- Better support for advanced patterns like reusable mappers, repositories, or DSLs  
- Improved readability and maintainability when extending generic base classes  

---

### 2. What shortcomings exist with current approaches?

Currently, there's no way to access a generic type parameter from the base class inside subclass methods or properties without repeating it explicitly.

For example, if a class extends:

```ts
class Mapper<TLocal, TRemote, TOptions> { ... }
```

And you write:

```ts
class UserAddressMapper extends Mapper<UserAddress, UserAddressFirestoreData, { userId: string }>
```

You cannot reference the ```TOptions``` parameter later in a method like:

```ts
getCollectionPath(options: this["TOptions"]) // ❌ Error
```

or perhaps:

```ts
class Mapper<TLocal, TRemote, TOptions> {
  type TOptions = TOptions; // exposed to subclasses
}

class UserAddressMapper extends Mapper<UserAddress, UserAddressFirestoreData, { userId: string }> {
  getCollectionPath(options: super.TOptions): string { ... }
}
```
Instead, you are forced to repeat the full type or extract it manually into a named type like:

```ts
type UserAddressMapperOptions = { userId: string };

```
 And Then:

```ts
class UserAddressMapper extends Mapper<..., ..., UserAddressMapperOptions> {
  getCollectionPath(options: UserAddressMapperOptions) { ... }
}
```

These workarounds:

- Introduce redundancy and boilerplate
- Reduce encapsulation
- Require exporting types that may be internal to a class
- Break down when working with inline or anonymous generic types

This is especially limiting in real-world patterns like declarative data mappers or repositories, where generics define behavior, and reuse across many subclasses becomes tedious without this.

### 3. What workarounds are you using in the meantime?

The only viable workaround today is to repeat the generic type manually:

```ts
type UserAddressMapperOptions = { userId: string };

export class UserAddressMapper extends Mapper<
  UserAddress,
  UserAddressFirestoreData,
  UserAddressMapperOptions
> {
  getCollectionPath(options: UserAddressMapperOptions): string {
    if (!options || !options.userId) {
      throw new Error("UserAddressMapper: userID is required in options to get collection path.");
    }
    return `users/${options.userId}/addresses`;
  }
}
```
This works but defeats the purpose of having a generic system that should enable reuse without duplication.

