A pick keyword for generic which may be an alternative to the extends keyword so that you can force exact matches. Meaning that if you were to request a generic `<T picks ObjectA | ObjectB>`, it has to be either `ObjectA` or `ObjectB` without it possibly being a union, intersection, extension of, etc.

### 📃 Motivating Example

A few examples where this may improve workflow is the following:

## Keyof Usage

Current:
```ts
interface Type {
    foo: number[];
    bar: string[];
}

class TypeStorage {

    private readonly storage: Type;

    constructor() {
        // ...
    }

    public set<K extends keyof Type>(key: K, value: Type[K][number]) {
        this.storage[key].push(value);
        //                     ^^^^^ Error
    }
}
```

Picks keyword:
```ts
interface Type {
    foo: number[];
    bar: string[];
}

class TypeStorage {

    private readonly storage: Type;

    constructor() {
        // ...
    }

    public set<K picks keyof Type>(key: K, value: Type[K][number]) {
        // Now type guarded as it can no longer be a union of
        this.storage[key].push(value);
    }
}
```

## Callback Parameters

Current:
```ts
function withBody(callback: (body: any) => any) {
    // ...
}

function withoutBody(callback: () => any) {
    // ...
}

function registerEndpoint<C extends typeof withBody | typeof withoutBody>(endpoint: C, requestCallback: Parameters<C>[0]) {
    endpoint(requestCallback);
    //       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ Error
}
```

Picks keyword:
```ts
function withBody(callback: (body: any) => any) {
    // ...
}

function withoutBody(callback: () => any) {
    // ...
}

function registerEndpoint<C picks typeof withBody | typeof withoutBody>(endpoint: C, requestCallback: Parameters<C>[0]) {
    // Now type guarded against unions
    endpoint(requestCallback);
}
```


## Reassembling Objects

Current:
```ts
interface Entry {
    id: number;
}

interface WebsiteEntry extends Entry {
    url: string;
}

interface UserEntry extends Entry {
    name: string;
}

interface SimpleWebsiteInfoEntries {
    websites: WebsiteEntry;
    users: UserEntry;
}

type WebsiteInfoEntries = {
    [K in keyof SimpleWebsiteInfoEntries]: SimpleWebsiteInfoEntries[K][]
};

class Repository<K extends keyof SimpleWebsiteInfoEntries, I extends Omit<SimpleWebsiteInfoEntries[K], keyof Entry>> {

    private readonly entries: WebsiteInfoEntries[K];

    constructor() {
        // ...
    }

    public addEntry(input: I) {
        this.entries.push(Object.assign({
            id: this.entries.length
        }, input));
        // ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ Error
    }
}
```

Pick keyword:
```ts
interface Entry {
    id: number;
}

interface WebsiteEntry extends Entry {
    url: string;
}

interface UserEntry extends Entry {
    name: string;
}

interface WebsiteInfoEntries {
    websites: WebsiteEntry[];
    users: UserEntry[];
}

class Repository<K picks keyof WebsiteInfoEntries, I picks Omit<WebsiteInfoEntries[K][number], keyof Entry>> {

    private readonly entries: WebsiteInfoEntries[K];

    constructor() {
        // ...
    }

    public addEntry(input: I) {
        // I is now guaranteed to be the same as the original without the id field so this is now safe
        this.entries.push(Object.assign({
            id: this.entries.length
        }, input));
    }
}
```

### 💻 Use Cases

1. Simplifying the usage of generics without weird workaround types or casts.
2. Usually it results in unreadable or slightly unsafe code making it harder to maintain.
3. Mapped types for the first example, inferring the arguments for the second and casts for the third.

