### 🔍 Search Terms

decorators, loops, concurrency, promises

### ✅ Viability Checklist

- [x] This wouldn't be a breaking change in existing TypeScript/JavaScript code
- [x] This wouldn't change the runtime behavior of existing JavaScript code
- [ ] This could be implemented without emitting different JS based on the types of the expressions
- [ ] This isn't a runtime feature (e.g. library functionality, non-ECMAScript syntax with JavaScript output, new syntax sugar for JS, etc.)
- [x] This isn't a request to add a new utility type: https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/wiki/No-New-Utility-Types
- [x] This feature would agree with the rest of our Design Goals: https://github.com/Microsoft/TypeScript/wiki/TypeScript-Design-Goals

### ⭐ Suggestion

I want to request a @Concurrent decorator for for/for...await/while loops. Under the hood it would wrap it with Promise.all/Promise.allSettled, and it would have a concurrency param for easy batching, so I wouldn't need to rewrite concurrency logic in all the projects.

Proposed usage:
```
@Concurrent()
for(const task of tasks) {
  await task.run();
}
```
this would execute them all concurrently.
```
@Concurrent({ settle: true, batch: 16 })
for(const task of tasks) {
  await task.run();
  await service.notify();
}
```
this would split tasks in batches of 16, and use Promise.allSettled in case of errors.

I am aware this is more of syntax sugar for Promise.allSettled

### 📃 Motivating Example

Instead of writing this:
```
export const sleep = async (time: number) =>
  new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, time));

export const withConcurrency = async (
  operations: ((...args: any[]) => Promise<any>)[],
  pLimit = 5,
  duration = 2000,
) => {
  do {
    await Promise.allSettled(operations.splice(0, pLimit).map((op) => op()));
    console.log('Batch filled. Sleeping ' + duration + ' ms');
    await sleep(duration);
  } while (operations.length);
  console.log('Finished!');
};

```
I could just use 
```
@Concurrent({ settle: false, batch: 5 })
for(const task of tasks) {
  await task.run();
  await service.notify();
  await sleep(2000)
}
```
so the code would be cleaner

### 💻 Use Cases

1. What do you want to use this for? Everything that requires large amount of concurrent operations. Think scanning large folders of files, big data, web scraping, splitting large tasks into smaller ones, etc 
3. What shortcomings exist with current approaches? Having to rewrite concurrency utils for each TS API I do
4. What workarounds are you using in the meantime? Native Promise.allSettled/Promise.all/for await combinations for batching

